  "id": "nutzen-benutzen",
  "name": "Make or do",
  "level": "B1",
  "status": "geprueft",
  "subtitle": "Make and do are easy to mix up. Use make when you create or produce something: make a cake, make a mistake. Use do for activities, work and tasks: do homework, do the shopping.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "make",
    "do",
    "create",
    "activities"
  ],
  "source": "icanspeak English original English text (grammar-b1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "exampleHtml": "I <span class=\"vp-verb\">made</span> a cake for Amira's birthday. · Tom <span class=\"vp-prep\">does</span> his homework after dinner. · Could you <span class=\"vp-prep\">do</span> me a favour?",
    "qa": [
      {
        "tag": "make = create",
        "html": "Use <strong>make</strong> when you create or produce something new. There is a result: a cake, a plan, a noise. <em>I made a cake. She made a decision. The children made a lot of noise. He wants to make friends. She makes good money.</em>"
      },
      {
        "tag": "do = activities",
        "html": "Use <strong>do</strong> for activities, work and tasks. The action is the main thing. <em>I do my homework. We do the shopping on Saturday. She is doing a course. He does exercise every morning. Do your best! Can you do me a favour?</em>"
      },
      {
        "tag": "Learn the pairs",
        "html": "Many of these are fixed phrases. You cannot work them out, so learn the verb and the noun together. <em>make a mistake</em> (✗ do a mistake), <em>do your best</em> (✗ make your best), <em>make a phone call</em>, <em>do the washing-up</em>."
      },
      {
        "tag": "do for general things",
        "html": "We use <strong>do</strong> when we speak in general and don't name the activity: <em>What are you doing? I did nothing yesterday. Let's do something fun.</em> And <em>do</em> is the verb for the housework: <em>do the cleaning, do the washing, do the ironing</em>. But: <em>make the bed</em>."
      },
      {
        "tag": "Typical mistakes",
        "html": "✗ <em>I did a mistake.</em> → ✓ <em>I made a mistake.</em><br>✗ <em>I make my homework every evening.</em> → ✓ <em>I do my homework every evening.</em><br>✗ <em>Can you make me a favour?</em> → ✓ <em>Can you do me a favour?</em>"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Some phrases look like they could take either verb, but only one is right: <em>make an effort</em>, <em>do business</em>, <em>make progress</em>, <em>do research</em>. A tip: ask yourself, <em>Do I create something new, or do I carry out an activity?</em> Create = make, activity = do. It does not work every time, so learn the common phrases. <em>Make</em> also has a different use with people: <em>make someone laugh</em>. See <a href=\"/make-let-help\">Make, let and help</a>. <em>Do</em> is also a helping verb in questions and negatives (<em>Do you like tea? I don't know.</em>). See <a href=\"/present-simple\">Present simple</a>.",
    "reference": {
